3. Baltimore, MD: In Baltimore, communities of color are disproportionately affected by environmental
hazards such as lead paint and air pollution. A study found that children in these neighborhoods have higher
blood lead levels, which are linked to lower IQ scores and increased behavioral issues. For example, Freddie
Gray, a young African American man who was murdered by Baltimore Police, experienced childhood lead
poisoning. [3]
4. Washington, DC: In Washington, DC, low-income neighborhoods often face higher exposure to pollutants
from traffic and industrial activities. Additionally, with climate change struggles such as an increase in
extreme heat, cumulative effects of these exposures contribute to higher rates of asthma and other chronic
diseases among residents, particularly among children and the seniors. [4] 
Intergenerational Considerations
The consideration of cumulative impacts is particularly crucial when examining maternal health and birth
outcomes. Research indicates that environmental stressors can adversely affect maternal health, leading to
complications such as preterm births and low birth weights. Like in Cancer Alley in Louisiana where
researchers found that newborns experience low birth weights at three times the national average. In
general, women living in areas with high levels of air pollution are at a greater risk for adverse pregnancy
outcomes. [5]
Groundbreaking Local Successes
There are notable examples where the consideration of cumulative impacts has led to successful
environmental decision-making at the local and state levels:
Los Angeles, CA: The city implemented the “Health and Equity in All Policies” initiative, which integrates
health considerations into all city planning and decision-making processes. This approach has led to
improved air quality and reduced health disparities in vulnerable communities. [6]
State of New York: The New York State Senate Bill S8830, also known as the Cumulative Impacts Law,
was passed in April 2022 and signed into law in December 2022. The law aims to reduce pollution
burdens on disadvantaged communities. The law requires that the State Department of Environmental
Conservation consider the overall pollution burden on disadvantaged communities when reviewing
permit applications. [7]
