and result in a far greater level of toxicity than the same amounts ac0ng alone. Ethanol and 
carbon tetrachloride are one example in which the toxicity of the combina0on is far greater 
than it would be if each chemical was tested separately. 
 
Small par0cles of solids are known to cause severe lung inﬂamma0on even if the solids are of a 
material that would be expected to have generally low chemical reac0vity. Par0cles that have 
adsorbed toxic chemicals have frequently been found to present an even greater danger. This 
raises the ques0on whether exposure to par0cles with simultaneous exposure to toxic or 

carcinogenic vola0le aroma0cs might display a pronounce synergis0c eﬀect. To date, there 
seems to be liPle data on this issue. 
 
One issue that is par0cularly important now is the ques0on of what happens when to 
neighborhoods already burdened with high levels of par0culates, ozone, NOX and SOX become 
sites for processing and/or liquefying natural gas. Areas where these processes are carried out 
are recognized as having high levels of asthma, heat and lung diseases and various cancers. The 
ques0on is if the results of adding compounds including benzene, toluene, ethyl benzene, and 
other aroma0cs are simply addi0ve or whether there may be synergis0c eﬀects that will greatly 
increase the health burden already faced by the residents. 
 
Some data that could help elucidate the eﬀects of diﬀerent stressors might be obtained by 
looking at the health problems in communi0es near sites where there is hydraulic fracturing to 
release oil or gas in shale deposits. Numerous studies on health issues in surrounding 
communi0es are now available. A few studies have also been done on households which are in 
close proximity to compressors used in natural gas lines. They show increased levels of rashes, 
asthma and other symptoms consistent with exposure to aroma0c hydrocarbons. It should be 
noted that people living near compressor sta0ons also complain about stress from the noise of 
the compressor, lights and worry that there may be a large release of vapors resul0ng in an 
explosion. These stressors are, of course, common for those living near heavy industry. The rural 
inhabitants near compressor sta0on generally have far less exposure to NOX, ozone and 
