Re: Comment on EPA’s Interim Framework for Advancing Consideration of Cumulative 
Impacts (Docket ID: EPA-HQ-OLEM-2024-0360) 
The principles and the process proposed by the EPA in its framework for Advancing 
Consideration of Cumulative Impacts are essential for the optimization of EPA’s mission to 
protect human health and the environment, while also critical for addressing the urgent needs the 
communities surrounding the Duwamish River face due to its numerous sources of pollution and 
health stressors. Due to its longstanding commitment to the Duwamish Valley, the Duwamish 
River Community Coalition is pleased to provide the following comments on the framework. 
The Duwamish Valley (DV) neighborhoods, part of the 98108 zip code, face poorer air quality 
than the rest of Seattle because of higher traffic related air pollution and other industrial 
emissions 1,2,3. Situated along the Duwamish River, a Superfund site resulting from decades of 
unmonitored and unregulated industrial activity, and bordered by 3 high traffic highways, 
residents of the 98108 zip code have 40% higher rates of childhood asthma hospitalizations, 
some of the highest heart disease mortality rates in the city, and life expectancies 13 years shorter 
than residents in wealthier areas of Seattle.4  
Residents that face these differences in health are mostly non-white; approximately 70% of 
residents in the 98108 zip code are Asian, Pacific Islander, Hispanic, African American, and 
Native American.5 
