Our organization, the Duwamish River Community Coalition, aims to address these 
discrepancies at our local level through environmental law and policy work, youth empowerment 
programing, and general community empowerment. Yet, in the past and presently, our residents 
that face the mentioned health disparities and risk factors cannot count on comprehensive 
frameworks at the federal level to address the cumulative impacts they experience. 
Opportunely, the principles set forth in this framework will consider the cumulative health risks 
in pollution and other risk factors, will pave the way for preventing new pollution sources from 
worsening existing disparities, and open the door for impact statements that consider cumulative 
impacts to become standard in permitting. 
Due to this framekwork’s inceptive nature, we have five suggestions for its improvement: 
1. We support the development of a framework that considers the evaluation of past and 
present cumulative impacts on health to actionably improve past cumulative harms that 
continue to impact our community today. Currently, there is not enough centralized work 
at the federal level to remediate cumulative impacts that our community faces today. 
Programs like the Comprehensive Environmental Response, Compensation, and Liability 
Act (CERCLA) establish prohibitions, requirements, and remediation for hazardous 
waste sites, however there are no optimized federal programs that remediate the other 
cumulative risks that continue to impact our families’ health and quality of life. This lack 
of action will continue even under the framework. Again, our community faces three high 
traffic highways, industries, the port of embarkation, and two airports, none of which are 
scrutinized collectively for their impacts on our lives or remediated for their pollution 
impacting our health. 
2. We support the inclusion of social determinants of health in this framework. Poverty, lack 
of education, lack of healthcare access, linguistic isolation, unemployment and poor 
housing conditions all contribute to health impacts in our community. Failing to address 
these factors in this framework and in any cumulative impacts analysis will fail to fully 
characterize our cumulative impacts. Including social determinants of health in impact 
assessments, and any framework, will ensure more accurate and focused policies that will 
more comprehensively address our health stressors and those around the nation while  
optimizing EPA’s mission to protect human health and the environment. 
3. We support interagency collaboration for addressing cumulative impacts. Ensuring all 
federal agency actions are scrutinized by their long-term and large-scale effects on health 
and the environment requires full agency commitment. A siloed approach from only the 
EPA will fail to use the resources, data, tools, frameworks, and skills from other agencies 
to address cumulative impacts most effectively and efficiently.  
4. We do not support the optional use of this framework. Applying a fit-for-purpose 
approach in this framework is essential, however if this fit-for-purpose framework is only 
an option, our community will still face their cumulative impacts without the use of the 
principles and processes set forth in this framework. 
5. We support the expedient and effective implementation of this framework and other 
frameworks for addressing cumulative impacts. Our communities currently face higher 
 

 
pollution impacts than other communities near us and will continue to unless there is a 
centralized approach to addressing cumulative impacts. The federal government is 
currently failing in its mission to protect human health and the environment in the 
Duwamish Valley community, as numerous risk factors feed into the health discrepancies 
experienced. The sooner a cumulative health framework is used the sooner human health 
and the environment will be protected in the Duwamish Valley. 
At the Duwamish River Community Coalition we aim to center community voices in decisions 
that affect their health. Currently, individual pollutant and risk factor thresholds at the federal 
level, like the NAAQS, fail to take a holistic view of our community and it is gravely impacting 
our quality of life, and health.  
The proposed framework would begin to address the increased risk we face holistically, from our 
most vulnerable infants with asthma to our elderly with chronic health diseases. There are no 
existing frameworks that do the same. An optimized approach that is centralized by the EPA and 
addressed with other federal agencies may begin to contribute to the years of life lost and quality 
of life impacted in the DV communities by cumulative impacts. 
 
We are thankful for the consideration! 
 
 
 
