Subject: Comments on Interim Framework for Advancing Consideration of Cumulative Impacts
Docket ID: EPA-HQ-OLEM-2024-0360
Pacific Environment appreciates the opportunity to offer our comments on the Environmental Protection 
Agency’s (EPA) Docket ID No. EPA-HQ-OLEM-2024-0360 Interim Framework for Advancing 
Consideration of Cumulative Impacts. Pacific Environment is a non-profit working to confront our most 
urgent environmental issues by connecting local and global movements, catalyzing policy change and 
inspiring action for the benefit of people and our planet. 
Pacific Environment supports EPA’s development of a framework for consideration of cumulative 
impacts.
The EPA’s framework for addressing cumulative impacts is an improvement over the current approach, 
which has been ineffective because it involves setting standards and assessment for individual pollutants 
without considering the health effects from multiple exposures. Many communities around the country 
are living amongst multiple polluting industries and face exposure to multiple toxic chemicals. Unsafe 
and compounding, these impacts lead to multigenerational toxic exposures that impact not only current 
but future generations. We commend the EPA’s Interim Framework as a positive step to address 
cumulative impacts, and would like to highlight the following points: 
1.
Ensuring effective operationalization of cumulative impact assessment within the EPA.
We support two specific recommendations included in the EPA OIG report “The EPA Needs to Further 
Refine and Implement Guidance to Address Cumulative Impacts and Disproportionate Health E␀ects 
Across Environmental Programs” published in August 2023. Namely:

1) Increase and improve coordination between EPA programs to assess and address cumulative 
impacts in Agency decision-making, programs, policies, and activities; and,
2) Develop and implement performance measures to monitor progress in identifying and addressing 
cumulative impacts across EPA programs.  
The Framework should clarify how EPA will address the above recommendations to develop and 
implement cumulative impact assessment performance measures, internal coordination, and monitoring. 
2.
Community involvement must be prioritized to operationalize this framework. 
We additionally want to emphasize that given the points mentioned above, in order for the EPA to 
properly conduct a cumulative impact assessment for a given area, they must understand the full context 
of environmental injustice experienced by residents in that area including social determinants of health 
indicators, such as access to health care. In order to do this effectively, community involvement from the 
first stages of implementation is critical. 
A variety of tools and methods of outreach must be utilized to maximize public participation. This should 
include providing translation services in community languages, accessible communication demonstrating 
that residents have essential lived expertise, and otherwise reducing as many barriers as possible for 
participation.
We would again like to thank the EPA for the opportunity to comment on Docket No. Docket ID No. 
EPA-HQ-OLEM-2024-0360 Interim Framework for Advancing Consideration of Cumulative Impacts.
Respectfully, 
Dr. Kristen McDonald 
