B. Cumulative Impacts in Environmental Siting and Permitting 
 
 
Many of our States require private parties to produce cumulative impacts analyses when 
applying for a permit for a project or action that might impose disproportionate environmental 
impacts on a disadvantaged community. Those analyses must account for, among other things, 
environmental and public health stressors already borne by the affected community as a result of 
existing conditions and the potential of the proposed project or action to add to existing 
environmental and health burdens. By shedding light on environmental and health disparities, 
needs, and vulnerabilities, cumulative impacts analysis enables state agencies to prevent projects 
or actions that will exacerbate existing inequities, or ameliorate the harms thereof.   
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
