The opinions expressed herein are our own and do not necessarily reflect the views of Johns 
Hopkins University. 
 
Comment on the Interim Framework for Advancing Consideration of Cumulative Impacts 
(Docket ID No. EPA-HQ-OLEM-2024-0360; 89 Fed. Reg. 92125) 
The Environmental Protection Agency’s (EPA) effort to incorporate cumulative impacts into 
environmental decision-making is an important step forward in improving the health of 
communities across the US. Evaluating the current conditions in communities provides valuable 
context for regulatory decisions and promotes greater awareness of health disparities in 
environmental justice communities. The Interim Framework for Advancing Consideration of 
Cumulative Impacts (Framework) provides basic principles, definitions, and examples of 
existing work in overburdened communities that incorporate cumulative impacts. As faculty of 
the Bloomberg School of Public Health and the Whiting School of Engineering at the Johns 
Hopkins University with experience in measuring and characterizing community burdens 
associated with complex exposures to mixtures of environmental stressors, we offer the 
following comments on the Framework, emphasizing the importance of providing concrete 
guidance for federal, state, and local governments. We (1) highlight the need for better 
monitoring of air pollutants in disadvantaged communities to avoid underestimation of health 
risks, (2) encourage development of guidelines for when to approve or deny permits that require 
evaluation of cumulative impacts, and (3) recommend incorporation of science-based adjustment 
factors into cumulative impact assessments (CIA). Details on each recommendation to follow. 
1. EPA should invest in research efforts to improve pollutant monitoring in 
communities  
Accurate measurement of pollutant exposures is a critical input in assessing cumulative impacts 
faced by fenceline communities.  As part of the Hazardous Air Pollutant Monitoring and 
Assessment Project (HAP-MAP),[1] we measured ethylene oxide concentrations along with 
approximately forty other hazardous air pollutants in an area with a high density of 
petrochemical manufacturers in southern Louisiana, also known as “Cancer Alley.” We initially 
focused analysis on ethylene oxide due to it being a potent carcinogen and because regulatory 
based monitoring at typical outdoor concentration levels has been challenging to measure 
accurately. Using state-of-the-art techniques and a mobile laboratory, we measured air 
concentrations of ethylene oxide that exceeded modeled values in EPA’s AirToxScreen for all 
census tracts in our study. Based on our measurements, cancer risk estimates for this single air 

 
2 
pollutant were 10 times higher than those based on modeled ethylene oxide concentrations from 
AirToxScreen. The majority of estimated risks were above EPA’s maximum acceptable risk at 
remediated Superfund sites (10-4). When we add cancer risks from other measured air pollutants, 
the cumulative burden for residents of these communities further increases.  
In addition to revealing underestimated health risks in fenceline communities, our research 
underscores another major weakness in federal level efforts to assess cumulative impacts. 
Current screening tools may be unable to identify overburdened communities if chemicals like 
ethylene oxide are not measured accurately. Even at a very low percentage of total emissions 
(0.2%), ethylene oxide contributed over 65% of the hazard from all measured hazardous air 
pollutants in Louisiana’s industrial corridor.[1]  
The EPA can help reduce disproportionate risks in communities like Louisiana’s Cancer Alley by 
improving monitoring capabilities. The Framework describes EPA efforts to invest in 
development of innovative tools and protocols to “expand and enhance the EPA’s ability to 
characterize and address the burdens facing communities.” As part of this effort, we encourage 
EPA to invest some of these resources into improving the accuracy and precision of air 
monitoring for chemicals which dominate cancer and non-cancer risks for communities burdened 
by exposure to hazardous air pollutants.  
2. EPA should provide decision-making guidelines for defining when a community is 
overburdened based on the existing conditions reports used in CIAs for permitting   
Existing conditions reports provide a profile of a community using a range of environmental, 
health, and socioeconomic indicators and are assembled prior to a proposed project. Several 
states already require existing conditions reports to be completed as part of a cumulative impact 
assessment for air permitting in environmental justice communities. For instance, the 
Massachusetts Department of Environmental Protection promulgated cumulative impact analysis 
regulations for air permitting in March 2024.[2] The regulations require permit applicants to 
evaluate and characterize the existing conditions of the community with 33 environmental, 
health, and socioeconomic indicators. Applicants must also provide opportunities for meaningful 
public involvement throughout the air permitting process and fulfill required steps for public 
notice. Finally, air dispersion modeling of criteria air pollutants must be conducted, and the 
applicant must characterize risk of cancer and non-cancer health endpoints. 
Review and decisions on the final disposition of the air permit require that the above bulleted list 
be completed, and the risk thresholds are not exceeded. Risk thresholds take the form of hazard 
indices or the sum of hazard quotients from all expected hazardous air pollutant emissions. 
Given that current models underpredict ambient concentrations of hazardous air pollutants 
deriving from industry, additional examination of expected emission should be performed. 
Furthermore, the regulations do not describe how the existing conditions report or any input from 
community involvement will influence the outcome of the permitting decision beyond the results 
of the risk characterization. In other words, the applicant is required to provide specific 
documentation of the conditions in the fenceline community, but there is no guarantee that the 
documentation will be meaningfully considered or result in any additional mitigation steps to 
reduce impacts on the community.  

 
3 
Similarly, the Minnesota Pollution Control Agency (MCPA) requires applicants to complete a 
Cumulative Levels and Effects Analysis (similar to an existing conditions report) for air permits 
in a small geographically defined area near a Superfund site in South Minneapolis.[3] This 
analysis requires an evaluation of environmental health indicators in the community, air toxics 
modeling, and risk characterization. MCPA issues permits based on compliance with risk 
thresholds and NAAQS and does not have the authority to deny permits based on other criteria. 
However, a new law requires MCPA to develop criteria for requiring cumulative impacts analysis 
in air permitting.[4] It offers an opportunity to define circumstances “that establish an 
environmental or health impact as a substantial adverse impact” for use in decisions on permit 
issuance or denial.[4] Other states such as Pennsylvania,[5] Maryland,[6] New York,[7] and New 
Jersey,[8] are developing or have existing laws and regulations requiring some consideration of 
cumulative impacts for permits in environmental justice areas that could be enhanced with 
guidance on permit decision-making.   
EPA can help protect fenceline communities by developing federal guidelines to describe 
conditions that would trigger permit denials, even when the traditional approach to risk 
assessment indicates facility emissions will result in no excess risk. As described in the appendix 
of the Framework, the Chicago Department of Public Health (CDPH) denied a permit application 
for a scrap metal facility in a vulnerable Southeast side community of Chicago. The permit was 
denied based on the results of the existing conditions analysis, not the risk assessment which 
indicated no increased risk of adverse health effects.[9] We encourage the EPA to address 
inconsistencies in decision-making processes through the development of guidelines for a variety 
of permits including air, waste, mining, land application of biosolids, concentrated animal 
feeding operations, sewage, underground injection, or any other project that may affect 
surrounding communities.  
3. EPA should provide recommendations for adjustment factors for use in risk 
assessments that protect fenceline communities   
Traditional risk assessment continues to serve as EPA’s core science policy tool for 
characterizing the nature and magnitude of health risks. The Framework notes that “cumulative 
impacts assessment can employ key components of traditional risk assessment in combination 
with or as an input to cumulative impacts assessment (Fox et al., 2017).” Integrating risk into 
CIA provides an opportunity to protect vulnerable populations using science-based adjustment 
factors that account for the wide range of variability in human responses to chemical and 
nonchemical stressors. 
Studies on human variability in response to chemical exposures have demonstrated that the 
default adjustment factor (10X) used by EPA for intra-species variability is not sufficient to 
protect human health. For example, the California EPA Office of Environmental Health Hazard 
Assessment[10] and the World Health Organization’s International Programme on Chemical 
Safety[11] found chemical-specific adjustment factors of 30X and 42X, respectively, better reflect 
the range of human variability due to intrinsic factors such as age, health conditions, or 
metabolism. Building on this work, additional extrinsic variability factors have been 
recommended to address cumulative exposure to chemical and nonchemical stressors.[12] 
Extrinsic factors include stressors associated with socioeconomic status, nutrition, air pollution, 
and other external stressors that may be considered in a CIA.[12]  

 
4 
Addressing chemical and nonchemical exposures through the development of additional 
adjustment factors is key to integrating cumulative impacts described in existing conditions 
reports into risk assessment frameworks. Highlighted in Technical Guidance from EPA’s Office 
of Policy, an important first step to incorporating environmental justice in risk assessment is 
producing a comprehensive review of “each relevant dose-response function that includes an 
analysis of baseline risk variation across different population groups.”[13] This recommendation 
falls in line with suggestions to mine large epidemiologic studies (e.g., the Environmental 
influences on Child Health Outcomes Program) for estimates of variability in vulnerable 
subgroups, [12]  and would allow risk analysts to account for a wider range of risk distributions. 
EPA can help strengthen CIAs by funding research on development of extrinsic variability 
factors and providing criteria for their application. These criteria would further bolster decision-
making guidelines for federal, state, and local permitting programs.  
Thank you for the opportunity to provide comments on the Framework. We applaud EPA’s 
efforts to develop the science and tools necessary to operationalize cumulative impact 
assessment, and enthusiastically support the further refinement of resources presented in the 
Framework.  
 
 
  
