Assignment #2: Public Comment  
 
​
 
​
The Environmental Protection Agencies Interim Framework for Advancing 
Considerations of Cumulative Impacts is a crucial step in addressing environmental 
health disparities in the United States, especially as underserved communities 
increasingly face the impacts of the current climate crisis. I support these considerations 
to increase environmental justice and improve health outcomes for all. However, while 
this framework does bring attention to the role of cumulative impacts in shaping health 
outcomes and environmental health, these considerations are a small step in the right 
direction. The EPA must seek to consider more intentional future guidelines if they wish 
to serve the most impacted communities more equitably. These guidelines should be 
more enforceable and carry harsher penalties for those who do not comply while also 
prioritizing the input of the communities they serve.  
 
According to the EPA, Cumulative Impacts are the combined effects of stressors 
that seriously threaten the health, well-being, and quality of life outcomes of those 
impacted (EPA, 2024). These stressors accumulate across a person’s entire lifetime 
and are equally influenced by environmental, social, and economic conditions. 
Underserved communities nationwide are already more likely to be impacted by 
pollution, food insecurity, or limited access to health care and are even more impacted 
when these stressors work in tandem (Odoms-Young et al., 2024). As a result, it is often 
said that “your zip code is just as, if not more, important than your genetic code” when 
considering health outcomes (RWJF, 2009). Cumulative Impacts disproportionately 
impact low-income and underserved communities, as well as communities of color, 
which have historically been negatively impacted by environmental injustice. These 
communities are often associated with heavy industrial facilities and interstates or have 
been built near brownfields (Estien et al., 2024). These environmental inequities have 
serious implications for the health and well-being of the residents in these communities. 
Studies show that communities exposed to high levels of air pollution as well as other 
pollutants, experience higher rates of asthma and cardiovascular disease (Thurston & 
Rice, 2019). A prime example is Curtis Bay in Baltimore. Residents of Curtis Bay saw a 
25% increased mortality rate compared to the rest of Baltimore. Among these deaths, 
6% could be attributed to chronic lower respiratory disease (Baltimore City Health 
Department, 2022). Additionally, residents of Curtis Bay were two times more likely to 
be hospitalized for asthma than the Maryland state average (Kelly & Burkhart, 2017). 
These statistics shouldn’t come as a surprise, with the I-95 Interstate and the largest 
medical waste incinerator in the United States calling Curtis Bay home.  
 
Regarding the strengths and limitations of the Interim Framework, It’s reassuring 
to see that the framework acknowledges the importance of location concerning 

 
environmental health. As I have previously stated, it is evident that underserved 
communities face a disproportionate burden and exposure to pollution and toxins. A key 
strength of this framework is the EPA’s commitment to identifying opportunities for 
interventions to improve health and quality of life while advocating for equity. Another 
key strength is the goal of increased transparency and engagement for the communities 
negatively burdened by Cumulative Impacts. Looking back in history, it is often the case 
that communities and stakeholders were left out of the decision-making process, 
especially regarding the placement of industry or interstate highways.  However, these 
frameworks seek to right the wrongs of the past by using the knowledge of the local 
community to ensure that health and quality of life can be at the center of future 
interventions. However, aside from these strengths, this framework has limitations that 
should be addressed to mitigate cumulative impacts properly. For instance, while the 
importance of community engagement is highlighted, additional guidelines should be 
provided should be included to ensure an equitable approach is taken for gathering 
feedback. For example, not all communities may be as well informed as others, so it is 
important that we ensure that when seeking engagement, various methods are required 
to ensure maximum engagement. Another weakness is the weight of Cumulative 
Impacts on permit decisions. While the framework takes a clear stance in recognizing 
that location and communities suffer the most due to historical environmental injustice, it 
is unclear how current and historical injustices will weigh in the decision-making 
process. In the future, I suggest more detailed guidelines and procedures should clarify 
what exact actions will be taken into consideration when these decisions are made. 
Additionally, I believe the framework must discuss in greater detail what enforcement 
mechanisms will be used to ensure these guidelines are followed in future regulatory 
decisions. Without the use of binding regulations and clear enforcement actions, It’s 
hard to believe that programs will be held accountable for ensuring Cumulative Impacts 
are considered moving forward.  
 
The Interim Framework for Cumulative Impacts has the opportunity to make 
positive changes to environmental efforts in the United States. With the EPA 
acknowledging the role of Cumulative Impacts in negatively impacting health outcomes. 
This framework has the potential to ensure that environmental justice, equity, and the 
quality of life for stakeholders are accounted for, which is critical for underserved 
communities that have suffered for years from heavy pollution, food insecurity, and 
decreased healthcare access. These impacts have the potential to be seen for 
generations as we continue to battle the climate crisis more work may be done to 
address the impacts on the communities with the highest burden. This guidance by the 
agency will ensure that policy, permits, and enforcement can no longer overlook the 
communities that need the most attention and care. Another significant impact is the 
increased engagement and transparency due to the agency's commitment to fostering 
more trust with communities facing the worst Cumulative Impacts. As I have stated, 
these communities have seen generations of adults and children whose health 
outcomes have been negatively impacted by the lack of action from the agency tasked 
with protecting them.  
 

 
Overall, the EPA’s Interim Framework is a step in the right direction, but it must 
be strengthened and enforced to create real progress in addressing environmental 
health disparities. Recognizing cumulative impacts is just the first step, as communities 
will continue to bear the brunt of pollution and environmental harm without clear 
mandates or accountability measures. The EPA should take this framework beyond 
acknowledgment by ensuring that cumulative impact assessments are mandatory in all 
major regulatory decisions. Additionally, the framework must establish stronger 
enforcement mechanisms to ensure compliance. Agencies and industries should face 
clear consequences if they fail to integrate cumulative impact considerations into their 
decision-making. Public engagement must not just be collected but carefully included to 
ensure that historically marginalized communities have an actual seat at the table. 
Environmental injustice is a complex issue, and the EPA is not the only agency that 
must work to solve it. However, if the EPA can spearhead efforts to undo the wrongs of 
the past and continue to prioritize equity, others will soon follow.  
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 

 
