 SUBJECT:​
Interim Framework for Advancing Consideration of Cumulative Impacts (Docket 
ID No. EPA-HQ-OLEM-2024-0360)  
____________________________________________________________________ 
GreenLatinos and WE ACT for Environmental Justice (“WE ACT”) are pleased to submit public 
comment on the EPA’s Interim Framework for Advancing Consideration of Cumulative Impacts. 
We commend the EPA’s commitment to addressing this issue, which is essential to protecting 
public health and advancing equitable environmental outcomes for disproportionately burdened 
communities. 
As an active community of 1,431 Latine members from across the United States, GreenLatinos is 
dedicated to advancing environmental justice and equity for the Latine community. Our mission 
is to ensure that equitable environmental outcomes principles are embedded at all levels of 
governmental policies. We envision a future where all communities are free from 
disproportionate environmental burdens, free to breathe clean air, drink pure water, access clean 
transportation, and enjoy our majestic public lands, oceans, and waters. As an organization 
rooted in the lived experiences of frontline communities who suffer from cumulative impacts, we 
work to amplify the voices of those most impacted by environmental inequities. 
WE ACT is an environmental justice advocacy nonprofit with a headquarters in West Harlem 
and a Federal Policy Office in Washington DC. Our mission is to build healthy communities by 
ensuring that people of color and/or low-income residents participate meaningfully in the 
creation of environmental health and protection policies and practices. Since its founding in 
1988, WE ACT has worked to serve environmental justice communities in New York and across 
the country which have been and continue to be adversely affected by harmful infrastructure, 
pollution, and the inequitable enforcement of environmental laws. WE ACT is also the convener 
of the Environmental Justice Leadership Forum (EJLF), a nationwide coalition of over 40 
environmental justice organizations.  
GreenLatinos and WE ACT strongly support the EPA’s decision to incorporate cumulative 
impacts into its regulatory and policy framework. This step represents a crucial acknowledgment 
 

 
of the interconnected and compounding factors contributing to environmental injustices, 
particularly for low-income communities and communities of color. By addressing these 
overlapping environmental, health, and socioeconomic burdens, the EPA is taking an essential 
step toward ensuring that policies and regulations reflect the realities of historically underserved 
communities.  
To that end, our comments emphasize the key tenets of environmental justice and advocacy 
priorities outlined in the Latino Climate Justice Framework (LCJF).1 The LCJF is a 
comprehensive blueprint designed to guide decision-makers and historically underserved 
communities in advancing equitable environmental policy solutions Developed through an 
extensive multiyear process of partner and community input, the first edition launched in 
October 2022 in collaboration with 22 Latine-serving partner organizations. In October 2024, we 
released the second edition of LCJF, reflecting the continued commitment to advance the 
priorities of nearly 2,000 individuals and/or organizations who have endorsed the LCJF.   
 
The LCJF is laid on the foundation of the Environmental Justice Principles,2 which was 
established at the First National People of Color Environmental Leadership Summit held in 
1991. The principles speak to cumulative impacts by focusing on recognizing and addressing the 
combined effects of multiple environmental and non-environmental stressors on communities. 
They emphasize the need to consider the totality of exposures and their effects on health and 
quality of life outcomes, including factors such as pollution, socioeconomics, culture, and other 
social determinants of health. 
Addressing cumulative impacts is a key to addressing environmental justice. Through this work, 
the EPA has an opportunity to ensure that environmental, socioeconomic, and health factors are 
contributing to the betterment of all communities across the United States.  
Cumulative Impacts Disproportionately Affect Communities of Color 
Communities of color face numerous climate-related issues, from extreme heat affecting outdoor 
workers and poor air quality in neighborhoods near industrial facilities to increased vulnerability 
to natural disasters like hurricanes, floods, and wildfires. As such, cumulative impacts—the 
combined effects of multiple environmental, health, and socioeconomic stressors over time—are 
often most severe in communities historically burdened by systemic inequities. For example, 
residents in these areas frequently live closer to polluting industries, endure higher exposure to 
hazardous substances, and face barriers to accessing healthcare and other resources that mitigate 
environmental risks.3   
3 Banzhaf, S., Ma, L., & Timmins, C. Environmental Justice: The Economics of Race, Place, and Pollution; Found at: 
https://www.jstor.org/stable/26566983 
2 The Principles of Environmental Justice, 1991; https://www.ejnet.org/ej/principles.pdf  
1 Latino Climate Justice Framework, 2022; Found at: https://www.lcjf.greenlatinos.org/ 
2 

 
A WE ACT analysis of communities identified as “disadvantaged” by the Biden administration’s 
Justice40 program4 revealed the extent to which communities of color face higher levels of 
cumulative environmental and economic burdens. The Justice40 initiative aimed to ensure that 
40% of the total benefits from specific federal investments—spanning climate, clean energy, 
affordable and sustainable housing, and more—reach disadvantaged communities that have 
historically faced underinvestment and disproportionate pollution burdens. According to WE 
ACT’s analysis, white communities were far more likely to live in census tracts with fewer issues 
while Black and Latine communities were typically located in areas that displayed economic 
issues and multiple elements of pollution.5  In fact, racial disparities in cumulative impacts 
exposure have been identified in analysis of multiple datasets including the  US Council of 
Environmental Quality’s Climate and Economic Justice Screening Tool (CEJST), 
CalEnviroScreen, New York State Disadvantaged Communities criteria, and others.6,7 The 
persistence of this relationship is robust evidence that communities of color experience 
disproportionate cumulative burdens.   
Numerous studies have shown that race is the most influential factor in determining the mortality 
risk from air pollution as it leads to higher exposure to pollution and its effects are heightened by 
factors such as poverty, existing health conditions, and poor availability of healthcare and 
healthy housing.8,9,10  Therefore, tackling cumulative impacts is a matter of equitable 
environmental outcomes and a significant public health priority. Studies have consistently shown 
that exposure to multiple pollutants exacerbates health conditions such as asthma, cardiovascular 
disease, and cancer. This is particularly relevant for low-income and communities of color, 
where children and families disproportionately face these health risks. 
According to the American Lung Association’s 2024 State of the Air Report, overall people of 
color are 2.3 times more likely than white people to live in a county with failing grades for ozone 
and fine particulate matter (PM2.5) pollution.11 For instance, over 1 in 3 Latines in the U.S. live 
