in counties where the air quality does not meet EPA standards.12 Additionally, research from the 
EPA-funded Center for Air, Climate, and Energy Solutions has found that Latines, along with 
other people of color, are disproportionately exposed to PM2.5 across various income levels and 
regions in the U.S.13 Additionally, Latines are more likely to live in areas with extreme heat 
exposure, further compounding health risks such as heat-related illnesses and cardiovascular 
issues14. This mirrors what is experienced by many communities of color across the country.  
Beyond environmental hazards, Latine populations also experience high levels of chronic stress 
due to socioeconomic disparities, acculturation difficulties, and discrimination. Research 
indicates that cumulative lifetime adversity—including economic hardship, workplace stress, and 
ethnic discrimination—contributes to elevated rates of depression and anxiety among Latine 
immigrants.15  Furthermore, high allostatic load—an indicator of chronic physiological 
stress—has been linked to increased risks of obesity and metabolic disorders in Latine 
communities, exacerbated by environmental stressors and social determinants of health.16 
Similarly, chronic stress as a result of systemic racism contributes to negative health outcomes 
for Black people including mental health disorders, cardiovascular disease, hypertension, 
diabetes, and maternal mortality.17 These interwoven factors highlight the urgent need for public 
health interventions and policies aimed at addressing environmental justice, socioeconomic 
inequalities, and culturally tailored mental health support within communities of color. 
Strengthening the EPA’s Interim Framework for Advancing Consideration of Cumulative 
Impacts 
The EPA should further strengthen the draft Framework to ensure it best serves communities that 
have historically been under-resourced and overburdened by environmental injustices and 
cumulative impacts. The bulk of our comments will revolve around the Interim Framework’s 
process for considering cumulative impacts, including the steps listed around initiation, scoping 
and problem formulation, assessment and informing decisions.  
