To ensure the EPA’s cumulative impacts framework is truly equitable and just, the agency must 
embed meaningful community engagement at every stage of the process—from initiation to 
decision-making and implementation. Impacted communities must not be treated as passive 
recipients of environmental decisions but as active partners in shaping policies that directly affect 
their health and well-being. 
A foundational step in this process is ensuring that all engagement efforts are linguistically and 
culturally accessible. Without this, the very communities disproportionately burdened by 
cumulative impacts—low-income, immigrant, and communities of color—will continue to be 
excluded from the decision-making process. In 2019, 68 million people in the United States 
spoke a language other than English, with Spanish speakers making up 62% of that population.18 
Failure to provide equitable language access and engagement is not just an oversight—it is a 
systemic barrier that perpetuates environmental injustice. 
The lack of language access does more than limit participation in decision-making–it also 
restricts communities’ ability to understand and act upon environmental risks. Cumulative 
environmental exposures–such as air pollution, contaminated water, hazardous waste, and 
extreme heat– disproportionately impact communities of color and low-income populations. 
However, many of these communities face additional barriers to understanding risk information, 
accessing legal protections, and advocating for policy solutions due to limited linguistic and 
technical accessibility.  
The Latino Climate Justice Framework (LCJF)19 and WE ACT’s Community Engagement 
Brief20 provides clear guidance and best practices on how to dismantle these barriers to public 
participation. We urge the EPA to integrate the following practices into its cumulative impacts 
framework to ensure meaningful community engagement: 
●​ Interface with communities early and frequently throughout the cumulative impacts 
assessment  process 
●​ Conduct a thorough language needs assessment in impacted communities to identify and 
provide services in the languages spoken by residents. 
●​ Strive for multi-modal outreach strategies (ex. digital and print forms, door-to-door 
recruitment) that make information accessible to all  
●​ Mandate that all notices, communications, and key documents be translated into the 
dominant languages of affected communities. 
●​ Require accessibility services, including translation and interpretation, at all stages and 
documents of public participation processes.  
20 WE ACT, Community Engagement Brief, 
https://www.weact.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/Community-Engagement-Brief-092322-FINAL.pdf  
19 Latino Climate Justice Framework (LCJF) 2025-2028  (Pg. 50): https://www.lcjf.greenlatinos.org/.  
18 U.S. Census Bureau. Languages We Speak in the United States; 
https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2022/12/languages-we-speak-in-united-states.html 
5 

 
●​ Collaborate with local ethnic media to disseminate public notices directly to multilingual 
and multiethnic communities.  
●​ Providing technical assistance, training, and resources (ex. orientation materials) to help 
community members participate effectively in the assessment process. These must be 
easily understandable and translated into relevant languages.  
●​ Recognize that not every U.S. resident has citizenship; therefore, public forums should 
not be held in venues requiring state-issued ID to enter, which restricts participation 
based on age and citizenship status. 
●​ Eliminate the presence of Customs and Border Protection and Immigration and Customs 
Enforcement officers at public forums to encourage participation from undocumented 
persons and mixed-status families. 
●​ Ensure information is accessible to people with disabilities, providing American Sign 
Language interpretation, descriptions of images, and Americans with Disabilities Act 
(ADA)- compliant websites in multiple languages as needed.  
●​ Account for health conditions and disabilities that may limit in-person attendance (ex. 
providing hybrid and virtual options for public engagement.  
●​ Expand outreach beyond voting populations – include community members under age 18. 
●​ Offer wraparound services to promote robust community attendance  (ex. Transportation 
options, reimbursement of childcare costs, etc). 
●​ Create easy and accessible ways for community members and environmental justice 
groups to provide input (giving them appropriate time and advance notice) and convey 
how it will be used or impact the assessment.  
To create an equitable and effective cumulative impacts framework, EPA must explicitly 
recognize the link between language barriers, public participation, and environmental exposure 
pathways. 
2.​   Ensure public participation processes do not exploit participants 
For many years, EJ communities have been the subject of academic and agencies’ studies and 
government public participation processes. In many cases, these communities have given up their 
time and expertise for no remuneration, recognition or attribution. Thus, while WE ACT and 
GreenLatinos encourage meaningful engagement at all levels, this engagement must respect the 
indigenous knowledge of these communities. EPA and relevant agencies should ensure that all 
those involved in creating and maintaining the Framework’s structure, research recent prior 
relevant community engagement efforts. Reports and data should be incorporated from past 
engagements so the communities are not being asked over and over to participate in the same 
type of community participation process. These same communities should be remunerated for 
their time and expertise and compensated for expenses for participation. In addition, when 
appropriate, these same communities should be recognized and their expertise cited along with 
6 

 
the authors and the researchers. The results of all studies and actions should be publicized (in the 
relevant languages) in the participant communities.  
3.​
Prioritize Addressing Disproportionate Burdens 
Historic discrimination has spawned an unjust legacy in which Black, Indigenous, Communities 
of Color (BIPOC) and low-income communities bear the greatest burden21 of direct exposure to 
toxic pollutants and increased vulnerability to a changing climate. Communities of color in the 
U.S. are more likely than their white counterparts to live near and thus unduly impacted by 
nearly all emission sectors,22 from congested transportation hubs to polluting fossil fuel 
infrastructure, leading to significant increases in childhood asthma, premature birth, 
cardiovascular ailments, and other health problems.  
As described in EPA’s Draft Framework, tools such as the CEJST or state examples like the 
California CalEnviroScreen tool are helpful in providing an initial screening of which 
communities are most burdened by cumulative impacts. However, they must ensure an accurate 
representation of the lived experiences. The identification of communities impacted by multiple 
sources of economic and environmental burdens requires the use of datasets that accurately 
represent the extent to which a community is dealing with a particular issue and its subsequent 
impacts.  This is of particular importance in cumulative impact tools that rely on an index 
method (CalEnviroScreen) as opposed to a matrix method (CEJST) as index methods also 
require input datasets to be appropriately weighted.   
An example of a tool that uses improper data to the detriment of communities is the Equitable 
Transportation Community Explorer (ETC) which was developed by the Department of 
Transportation during the Biden administration to assist in implementation of Justice40. A WE 
ACT analysis of this tool found that in order to quantify risks from climate change it 
incorporated a FEMA dataset called Annualized Hazard Loss. While this dataset does account 
for increased risk of extreme weather disasters due to climate change, it also includes the 
property values. If you compared two communities within this dataset, one wealthy and one 
poor, at the same level of risk from extreme weather events, the wealthy community would 
receive a higher value within the dataset. Thus, this dataset serves to elevate wealthy 
communities over poor communities within the context of the ETC methodology which is the 
exact opposite of what should be done in a cumulative impacts tool.23 This error is further 
compounded in the methodology by the weighting applied in the final calculation. Due to this 
weighting, this data had more influence on the final score than all but one of the dozens other 
23 WE ACT Comment on Request for Information on US DOT Equitable Transportation Community Explorer (ETCE) 
Tool and Index Methodology, https://www.regulations.gov/comment/DOT-OST-2023-0020-0045  
22 University of Illinois, People of color hardest hit by air pollution from nearly all sources, 
https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2021/04/210428140906.htm  
21 PNAS. Air Pollution Exposure Disparities Across U.S. Populations; 
https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.1818859116 
7 

 
datasets incorporated into the tool. Including inappropriate datasets and making poor 
methodological choices can severely impact the end results in a cumulative impacts calculation 
and lead to inefficient or incorrect policy decisions. 
Once an initial screening tool is used, it is critical to utilize the methods described in the previous 
section around meaningful community engagement to get a greater sense of the experienced 
cumulative impacts in a particular community. We recommend that the EPA prioritize the 
following to more directly address disproportionate burdens: 
●​ In addition to exposure and environmental indicators, include social determinants of 
health (SDOH) such as poverty, education, healthcare access, linguistic isolation, and 
housing conditions in cumulative impacts analysis. Including SDOH in impact 
assessments ensures more accurate equity-focused policies that address both 
environmental and social stressors. The CalEnviroScreen tool24 provides a strong 
example which encompasses 21 indicators including:  
○​ Exposure Indicators - PM2.5, Lead, Diesel, Drinking Water, Pesticides, Toxic 
Release, Traffic. 
○​ Environmental Effect Indicators - Cleanups, Groundwater Threat, Hazardous 
Waste, Impaired Water Bodies, Solid Waste. 
○​ Sensitive Population Indicators - Asthma, Cardiovascular Disease, Birth Weight. 
○​ Socioeconomic Factor Indicators - Education, Housing, Linguistic Isolation, 
Poverty, Unemployment. 
●​ Acknowledge the historical context of environmental racism and discriminatory policies, 
and their ongoing impact on communities of color, including the exacerbation of 
cumulative impacts. 
●​ Integrate traditional and subsistence practices in environmental justice communities that 
create unique exposure pathways to environmental contaminants including: 
○​ Fishing in Contaminated Waterways – Consuming locally caught fish can expose 
individuals to mercury and PCBs, risks often underestimated in regulatory 
models.25 
○​ Farming and Pesticide Exposure – Farmworkers and nearby residents face 
repeated exposure through air drift, soil contamination, and residues.  
○​ Traditional Food Preparation – Cooking with open flames, clay cookware, or 
wood-burning methods can increase inhalation of harmful pollutants  
4. Learn from States and Other Jurisdiction’s Application of Cumulative Impacts   
As the EPA interim guidance outlined, several interested parties at the state and local levels have 
integrated cumulative impacts into their decision-making processes while others have a clear 
25 Washington State Department of Health. Contaminants in Fish; Found at: 
https://doh.wa.gov/community-and-environment/food/fish/contaminants-fish 
24California Office of Environmental Health Hazard Assessment (OEHHA). CalEnviroScreen 4.0 Indicators Overview; 
Found at: https://oehha.ca.gov/calenviroscreen/indicators-overview.  
8 

 
need for cumulative impacts analysis and would greatly benefit from federal guidance on the 
subject. Environmental justice and community based groups like GreenLatinos and WE ACT 
have long fought for consideration of cumulative impacts that ultimately leads to public health 
improvements and mandatory emission reductions26 in the low-income and communities of color 
we serve. Below we offer a further few examples of the assessment and/or consideration of 
cumulative impacts from our local constituency and state programs:  
 
Colorado 
Latine communities across Colorado, particularly in North Denver, Commerce City, and Pueblo, 
continue to bear the brunt of environmental injustice, as highlighted in recent reports on 
cumulative impacts. These historically marginalized neighborhoods face disproportionate 
exposure to industrial pollution, hazardous transportation infrastructure, and fossil fuel 
operations. The Environmental Justice Act (HB21-1266) seeks to address these cumulative 
impacts by strengthening air quality regulations, enhancing community engagement, and 
ensuring that fines from environmental violations are reinvested into impacted communities. The 
establishment of the Environmental Justice Action Task Force has created a pathway for Latine 
and other frontline communities to participate in shaping policies that address long-standing 
environmental health disparities. However, systemic barriers—such as language inaccessibility, 
inadequate data transparency, and insufficient enforcement of regulations—continue to hinder 
true environmental equity. 
In Pueblo, where the Latine population comprises a significant portion of the community, 
repeated EPA violations from industrial facilities correlate with high rates of asthma and other 
respiratory illnesses. Similar patterns emerge in North Denver and Commerce City, where 
polluting industries operate in close proximity to schools and homes, exacerbating health risks. 
Despite the documented injustices, gaps in data and inconsistent enforcement allow these 
environmental burdens to persist. Grassroots organizations like GreenLatinos CO and Mothers 
Out Front are actively working to empower Latine communities through education, advocacy, 
and policy reform. Their efforts emphasize the need for a just transition away from fossil fuel 
reliance and towards renewable energy solutions that prioritize community well-being. 
Addressing these environmental injustices requires a sustained commitment to holding polluters 
accountable, investing in localized clean energy alternatives, and ensuring that Latine voices are 
at the forefront of environmental decision-making. 
Illinois 
The Chicago Cumulative Impact Assessment (CIA), released in September 2023, examines how 
environmental, health, and social stressors disproportionately affect communities across the city. 
Conducted in collaboration with community leaders, the assessment found that Black and Latine 
26 Equitable and Just National Platform (2021), Approaches to Defining Environmental Justice Community for 
Mandatory Emissions Reduction Policy, 
https://www.weact.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/Defining-EJ-Community-for-Mandatory-Emissions-Reduction-Poli
cy.pdf  
9 

 
Chicagoans are disproportionately exposed to environmental hazards. They are more likely to 
live near toxic industrial sites, leading to higher levels of air pollution. In fact, 95% of Black and 
Latine residents live in areas that fail federal air quality standards. As a result, these communities 
face lower life expectancies and higher rates of cancer,  asthma, and heart disease. These 
neighborhoods also struggle with limited access to green spaces and clean water, further 
compounding the health disparities that already exist. These findings underscore the need for 
targeted interventions to address systemic inequities.   
 
The CIA directly influenced Chicago’s Environmental Justice Action Plan, ensuring resources 
and policies address the most impacted communities. In response, Mayor Brandon Johnson has 
prioritized a cumulative impacts ordinance to protect all residents from pollution, climate 
change, and harmful land use.   
 
The cumulative impacts ordinance is set to be introduced in the next few months and will do the 
following:  
 
●​ Require comprehensive assessments of environmental and health impacts for areas 
already facing pollution from multiple sources. 
●​ Shift zoning and land use policies to consider cumulative environmental burdens, 
particularly in vulnerable communities. 
●​ Prioritize health equity, ensuring that disadvantaged neighborhoods are protected from 
further environmental harm. 
●​ Improve public involvement in decisions affecting local environments and public health, 
particularly in communities of color. 
●​ Promote cleaner development practices by holding industries accountable for their 
collective impacts on the surrounding areas. 
 
GreenLatinos Illinois will collaborate with local community partners to educate residents about 
the ordinance and rally support from our alderpeople to ensure its passage through city council. 
 
Cumulative impact assessments must extend beyond Chicago. Similar studies across Illinois and 
its metropolitan areas are critical to uncovering environmental injustices statewide. Illinois is 
home to numerous areas with significant environmental hazards, particularly in low-income and 
communities of color, where residents are disproportionately exposed to industrial pollution, 
hazardous waste, and poor air quality. Conducting similar studies in rural and suburban regions is 
critical to identifying other communities that are disproportionately impacted. 
 
New Mexico 
In New Mexico, one of only a handful of “majority minority” states, the cumulative impacts of 
environmental toxins, racial injustice, and poverty are not well understood. With clear issues that 
10 

 
can be seen in a complaint against the City of Albuquerque and Bernalillo County that led to a 
federal civil rights investigation by the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency. 
(https://www.nrdc.org/sites/default/files/2024-06/nm-titlevi-complaint-jardines-20240530.pdf) 
 
This case is notable because this occurred in the population center of the State of New Mexico, 
but this does not take into account the myriad rural and tribal communities. These communities 
are often adjacent to sources of pollution, especially methane from oil and gas production as well 
as coal ash and hexavalent chromium from coal-fired power plants. The combination of these 
environmental factors, low wages, and limited services produce inequalities that are not well 
studied. Since the 1940s, our state has been considered a “sacrifice zone” and its people have 
suffered the consequences of this. As one of the poorest states in the country, New Mexico has 
lacked the resources to adequately study and address these cumulative impacts on Latine and 
Indigenous communities across the state. 
 
GreenLatinos has been working with our partners to support the reduction of environmental 
damage that impacts communities of color at a higher rate. We are also launching an educational 
tour of the state this year to inform Latine and Indigenous communities about the cumulative 
effects of the toxins in their air, water, and soil combined with socioeconomic and racial 
challenges. 
 
New York 
WE ACT,  along with another community based organization,  South Bronx Unite, successfully 
led a coalition campaign to pass New York’s cumulative impacts law27 in 2022. This law is the 
second in the nation, behind New Jersey,28 to pass permitting reform legislation that requires 
facilities to document their cumulative environmental and health impacts on disadvantaged 
communities, and the state to potentially deny permits if facilities are increasing cumulative 
burden. The law was enacted as of December 31st, 2024.29  
This landmark law has four components: 
●​ Regulates New Facilities, 
○​ Ensuring that no permits be issued if the project is determined by the New York 
State Department of Environmental Conservation (NYSDEC) to cause or 
contribute more than a de minimis amount of pollution to a disproportionate 
pollution burden on the disadvantaged community.  
●​ Regulates Existing Facilities  
29 WE ACT, Governor Hochul Signs Landmark Environmental Justice Legislation Reducing Cumulative Impacts of 
Pollution on Disadvantaged Communities, 
https://www.weact.org/2022/12/governor-hochul-signs-landmark-environmental-justice-legislation-reducing-the-cumul
ative-impacts-of-pollution-on-disadvantaged-communities/  
28 New Jersey Environmental Justice Law, https://dep.nj.gov/wp-content/uploads/ej/docs/ej-law.pdf  
27 NY State Senate Bill S1317, https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/bills/2023/S1317  
11 

 
○​ For a renewal facility, a permit will not be  issued if it would significantly 
increase the existing disproportionate pollution burden in the disadvantaged 
community.  
●​ Requires Reporting Mechanisms 
○​ Directs NYSDEC to require permit applicants (i.e. facilities covered under this 
legislation) to prepare an “existing burden report” for a new project, applicable 
permit modification, or renewal of a permit. 
●​ Reduces Pollution  
○​ Directs NYSDEC to require actions for a facility to implement any appropriate 
operational changes that would reduce the pollution burden on the disadvantaged 
community as a condition of an applicable permit. 
Campaign efforts continue as the NYSDEC has recently released draft regulations to implement 
the law. The regulations include proposed amendments to the State Environmental Quality 
Review Act to explicitly include the evaluation of cumulative impacts. The coalition is actively 
involved in the public comment process. Stringent regulations that include highly protective 
definitions of pollution burdens and incorporation of affected communities into the 
decision-making as well as strong internal guidance, and oversight from the Office of 
Environmental Justice in their implementation are critical to the success of the New York State 
cumulative impacts law. 
Conclusion 
There is perhaps no single greater issue in the world of environmental justice than that of 
cumulative impacts.  In order to fully address the public and environmental health of 
communities, a comprehensive and holistic approach that accounts for the environmental, 
economic, and social health of a community is necessary to fully understand the impact of past 
and future actions on said community.  Environmental justice organizations have long pushed for 
policies and legislation that account for the full scope of pollution and environmental impacts on 
frontline communities.  Mountains of research, including but not limited to those studies cited 
within these comments, have shown that communities of color within the United States bear an 
immoral and unjust level of environmental and economic burdens and addressing these concerns 
requires full incorporation and a cumulative impacts approach into the Agency’s work. 
GreenLatinos and WE ACT for Environmental Justice reiterate that we commend the EPA for 
addressing this crucial issue and are thankful for the opportunity to provide comments.  We 
believe the feedback provided within these comments will make a finalized cumulative impacts 
framework stronger and we look forward to working with the EPA to ensure the health and 
safety of frontline communities and communities of color. 
Respectfully, 
GreenLatinos 
WE ACT for Environmental Justice 
 
